WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:22.020
<v Ira Glass>A friend of mine ran her own small business. It was just her and this guy, the bookkeeper. And she loved the bookkeeper. The bookkeeper did the billing. He kept the accounts. He saw that vendors were paid on time. He did basically all the stuff that, to my friend, was just kind of drudgery. She used to say that she didn't know what she would do without him. They were really close. So it was this great setup for years.
<v Ira Glass>我的一位朋友曾经营自己的小企业。公司里只有她和那个负责记账的人。她很信任那个记账员。记账员负责开账单、管理账户、确保供应商按时收到货款。基本上，她认为所有繁琐的工作都是由他完成的。她过去常说，没有他她不知道该怎么办。他们关系非常亲密。所以，多年来这是一种非常好的合作模式。

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:48.080
<v Ira Glass>And then one day, the bookkeeper disappeared with her money. Classic inside job. Perfect one. The bookkeeper knew everything. My friend totally trusted him. And sometimes I wondered, was the idea of using what he knew and taking the money like this little bug in his brain eating away at him for years. Once he figured out that he could take the money, was it hard for him not to take the money?
<v Ira Glass>后来有一天，记账员卷着她的钱消失了。经典的监守自盗。极其完美的一案。记账员了解一切。我的朋友完全信任他。有时我纳闷，利用他所知的内部信息去偷钱这个想法，是不是像个在他脑子里啃噬了多年的小虫子。一旦他意识到自己可以拿走这笔钱，再让他不这么做是不是很难？

00:00:48.080 --> 00:01:09.440
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our radio show, we have two stories of people who get inside. They get inside some system. They truly understand the system. And then they have to figure out what to do with their insider knowledge. One of the stories is this incredible tale of money guys on the make right when the housing market was starting to collapse. The other is this story of cops and robbers, drug busts and drug users playing a cat and mouse game.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的广播节目有两个关于“深入内部”的故事。人们潜入某个系统，真正了解这个系统，然后不得不面对如何利用这些内部知识的问题。其中一个故事是关于住房市场开始崩溃时，金融从业者大肆敛财的不可思议的故事。另一个则是关于警察与罪犯、毒品扫荡与吸毒者之间玩着猫鼠游戏的故事。

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:24.660
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥 WBEZ 电台，我是 Ira Glass，正在为您播出由公共广播国际（Public Radio International）发行的《美国生活》（This American Life）。请继续收听。

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:33.420
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Eat My Shorts. Something you hear from a lot of people on Wall Street, this crisis that we're going through now, nobody saw it coming.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，吃我的短裤（Eat My Shorts）。华尔街许多人经常说的一句话是，我们现在经历的这场危机，没人预见到它会发生。

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.170
<v Angelo Mozilo>Nobody saw this coming.
<v Angelo Mozilo>没人预见到会发生这种事。

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:42.360
<v Ira Glass>See what I mean? This is Angelo Mozilo, the former CEO of the failed mortgage lender Countrywide on Australia Broadcasting.
<v Ira Glass>明白我的意思吗？这是倒闭的抵押贷款机构 Countrywide 的前首席执行官 Angelo Mozilo 在澳大利亚广播公司（Australia Broadcasting）上的言论。

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:51.250
<v Angelo Mozilo>S&P and Moody's didn't see it coming. Bear Stearns certainly didn't see it coming. Merrill Lynch didn't see it coming. Nobody saw this coming.
<v Angelo Mozilo>标普（S&P）和穆迪（Moody's）没预见到；贝尔斯登（Bear Stearns）肯定没预见到；美林证券（Merrill Lynch）也没预见到。没人预见到这一点。

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.870
<v Ira Glass>Brian Moynihan, head of Bank of America, told a congressional hearing he didn't either.
<v Ira Glass>美国银行（Bank of America）总裁 Brian Moynihan 在国会听证会上也表示他没预见到。

00:01:54.870 --> 00:02:05.580
<v Brian Moynihan>No one involved in the housing system, lenders, rating agencies, investors, insurers, consumers, regulators, and policy makers foresaw a dramatic and rapid depreciation in home prices.
<v Brian Moynihan>住房系统中的任何人，无论是贷款机构、评级机构、投资者、保险公司、消费者、监管机构还是政策制定者，都没有预见到房价会出现剧烈且迅速的贬值。

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:13.070
<v Ira Glass>Even Robert Rubin, former treasury secretary, former chairman of the executive committee of Citigroup's Board of Directors said just this week--
<v Ira Glass>甚至连前财政部长、花旗集团董事会执行委员会前主席 Robert Rubin 本周都说——

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:32.480
<v Robert Rubin>Almost all of us, including me, who were involved in the financial system, that is to say, financial firms, regulators, rating agencies, analysts, and commentators, missed the powerful combination of factors that led to this crisis and the serious possibility of a massive crisis.
<v Robert Rubin>我们中几乎所有人，包括我在内，那些参与金融体系的人，也就是金融公司、监管机构、评级机构、分析师和评论员，都错过了导致这场危机的强大因素组合，以及发生大规模危机的严重可能性。

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:51.340
<v Ira Glass>The recent collapse of the financial system has been described as a 100-year flood, a perfect storm, a force of nature. And it is so frustrating to hear it described that way, as something that happened to Wall Street instead of something that Wall Street brought on itself.
<v Ira Glass>近期的金融体系崩溃被描述为百年一遇的洪水、完美风暴、自然力量。听到这种描述让人感到非常沮丧，仿佛这是发生在华尔街身上的事，而不是华尔街自找的。

00:02:51.340 --> 00:03:18.930
<v Ira Glass>At the height of the boom, Wall Street was doing such strange, dangerous things, bankers and brokers all over the country were giving huge mortgages to people who could not afford to pay them back. Investors were then buying and selling these terrible mortgages to each other as if they were worth something. Banks convinced analysts to rate this stuff as completely safe. And then they believed their own lousy ratings. They believed their own hype. And really, nobody suspected this could all come crashing down?
<v Ira Glass>在繁荣的顶峰时期，华尔街正在做着如此奇怪、危险的事情：全国各地的银行家和经纪人都在向那些根本无力偿还的人发放巨额抵押贷款。然后，投资者又像这些贷款很有价值一样，把这些糟糕的抵押贷款买来卖去。银行说服分析师将这些东西评为绝对安全。然后，他们相信了自己拙劣的评级。他们相信了自己的炒作。难道真的没人怀疑这一切会崩溃吗？

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:48.540
<v Ira Glass>Here on our radio show, we have never believed that this was true. We've never believed this idea that nobody knew. That really, we're all to blame because all of us, from homeowners to investment bankers, all got caught in this irrational exuberance in the housing bubble. We didn't believe it. After two years of reporting on this crisis, we felt pretty sure that not only must there be some people on Wall Street who figured out what would happen, there must have been people who helped bring on the crisis, who fed the fire, and who figured out ways to profit from that.
<v Ira Glass>在我们的广播节目中，我们从未相信这是真的。我们从未相信“没人知道”这种说法。我们认为我们都有责任，因为我们所有人，从房主到投资银行家，都陷入了住房泡沫中那种非理性的繁荣。我们不相信。在报道这场危机两年后，我们相当确定，华尔街不仅肯定有人预见到了会发生什么，而且肯定有人推波助澜，助长了这场火势，并找到了从中牟利的方法。

00:03:48.540 --> 00:04:10.890
<v Ira Glass>And so we wanted to find some of those people. To do that, we and our regular team of crack economics reporters at Planet Money asked the investigative reporting outfit ProPublica to join up with us. ProPublica spent seven months conducting dozens of interviews, pouring over thousands of pages of financial documents. And they uncovered some things no one has ever heard before in all the reporting that's been done on the crisis.
<v Ira Glass>所以我们想找到其中的一些人。为了做到这一点，我们和 Planet Money 节目的专业经济报道团队请求调查报道机构 ProPublica 与我们合作。ProPublica 花了七个月时间进行了数十次采访，查阅了数千页的财务文件。他们发现了一些在所有关于这场危机的报道中从未被提及的事实。

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:33.400
<v Ira Glass>And today, with their help, we bring you this story of people at one company and their associates who took home hundreds of millions of dollars in personal income while, at the same time, helping to worsen the crisis that taxpayers, that you and I, will be paying off for years to come. Here's Alex Blumberg with the reporting team from ProPublica, Jake Bernstein and Jesse Eisinger, to bring you the story of this inside job.
<v Ira Glass>今天，在他们的帮助下，我们将为您讲述一家公司的员工及其合伙人是如何在个人收入中赚取数亿美元，同时又加剧了这场将让纳税人，也就是你和我，在未来几年持续买单的危机。接下来是 Alex Blumberg 与 ProPublica 的报道团队 Jake Bernstein 和 Jesse Eisinger，为您讲述这个监守自盗的故事。

00:04:33.400 --> 00:05:00.780
<v Alex Blumberg>This inside job takes place deep in the heart of Wall Street financial engineering. It's full of intrigue and genius and questionable behavior. And it resembles, in ways that will eventually be revealed, the plot of a Mel Brooks musical. But it begins far from Wall Street, on the shores of Lake Michigan in Chicago, where a man named Alec Litowitz was starting a new hedge fund. We'll get to what exactly a hedge fund is in a minute. First though, here's Jake Bernstein from ProPublica with the hedge fund's name.
<v Alex Blumberg>这个监守自盗的故事发生在华尔街金融工程的核心地带。它充满了阴谋、天才和可疑的行为。而且，最终你会发现，它很像一部梅尔·布鲁克斯（Mel Brooks）的音乐剧的情节。但它始于远离华尔街的地方——芝加哥密歇根湖畔，一个叫 Alec Litowitz 的人正在筹建一个新的对冲基金。我们一会儿再来谈什么是对冲基金。首先，这是 ProPublica 的 Jake Bernstein 带来的关于这个基金名字的介绍。

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:17.330
<v Jake Bernstein>Magnetar. And that's because Litowitz, the guy who started Magnetar, is a big astronomy buff. A magnetar is kind of like a black hole. It's a star that's burned out. And it is the most magnetic force in the universe.
<v Jake Bernstein>Magnetar。那是因为创立 Magnetar 的 Litowitz 是个天文迷。Magnetar（磁星）有点像黑洞。它是一颗燃烧殆尽的恒星。它是宇宙中最强的磁力源。

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:18.930
<v Jesse Eisinger>And they have a marketing spiel.
<v Jesse Eisinger>而且他们有一套营销说辞。

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:24.740
<v Alex Blumberg>You may not have noticed, but that was the other half of the ProPublica duo entering the conversation, Jesse Eisinger.
<v Alex Blumberg>你可能没注意到，刚才加入对话的是 ProPublica 团队的另一位成员，Jesse Eisinger。

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:55.370
<v Jesse Eisinger>And they have a marketing spiel. They say that this firm is going to attract the best employees, the best investors. We're going to have the highest returns. And they give t-shirts to people that say, "Very bright. Very magnetic." And sometimes employees would joke that they're named after a black hole. But for the most part, they start out with these high ambitions and excitement, and they're really enthusiastic about it.
<v Jesse Eisinger>而且他们有一套营销说辞。他们说这家公司会吸引最优秀的员工、最优秀的投资者。我们将获得最高的回报。他们会给员工发放印有“非常聪明，非常有吸引力”（Very bright. Very magnetic.）的 T 恤。有时员工会开玩笑说，他们是以黑洞的名字命名的。但大多数时候，他们一开始就怀着远大的志向和兴奋，对此充满热情。

00:05:55.370 --> 00:06:28.730
<v Alex Blumberg>So what do hedge funds do anyway? Well basically, they gather a bunch of money from investors, and they try to get that money to grow using whatever strategy they think will make them and their investors rich. Magnetar decided that one of the ways it was going to make money was by investing in a corner of the financial markets we've heard a lot about lately, securities made of subprime mortgages, and was going to go for one of the more esoteric of these securities, collateralized debt obligations or CDOs. Such are the times that we live in that CDOs have appeared on more than one episode of This American Life lately.
<v Alex Blumberg>那么对冲基金到底是做什么的呢？简单来说，他们从投资者那里募集一笔钱，然后尝试通过他们认为能让他们和投资者致富的任何策略，让这笔钱增值。Magnetar 决定，他们赚钱的方式之一就是投资于我们最近常听到的金融市场的一个角落，即由次级抵押贷款组成的证券，而且他们打算选择这些证券中更深奥的一种，即担保债务凭证（CDO）。现在的时代就是这样，CDO 最近在《美国生活》节目中已经出现过不止一次了。

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:59.990
<v Alex Blumberg>CDOs have the distinction of being the single most toxic financial instruments of the financial crisis. The instruments that did more damage to the world's financial system than any other single instrument out there. And what you need to know about a CDO to understand this story is pretty simple. A CDO, at least the kind Magnetar was interested  in, is a financial security that's made up of dozens of bonds. And each bond is itself made up of hundreds of individual mortgages and not safe mortgages. These were pretty risky ones to borrowers with poor credit.
<v Alex Blumberg>CDO 被认为是金融危机中最有毒的金融工具。这些工具对全球金融体系造成的损害比其他任何单一工具都要大。要理解这个故事，关于 CDO 你只需要知道一点：CDO，至少是 Magnetar 感兴趣的那种，是一种由几十种债券组成的金融证券。而每一种债券本身又由数百笔个人抵押贷款组成，而且还不是安全的贷款。这些贷款对于信用较差的借款人来说是非常有风险的。

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:33.670
<v Alex Blumberg>Now Alec Litowitz, who was only 38 when he started Magnetar, had already amassed a personal fortune in finance. This allowed him and his wife to take a couple of years kicking around Europe, collecting antiques for a big house they were building on the shores of Lake Michigan. And during the time they were away, CDOs were becoming the hot, new thing on Wall Street. A record number were on their way to being sold in 2005, which would shatter the previous record set in 2004. But by the time Alec Litowitz was back and had his new hedge fund up and running at the end of 2005, things were starting to change in the CDO world.
<v Alex Blumberg>Alec Litowitz 在创立 Magnetar 时年仅 38 岁，他已经在金融界积累了个人财富。这使他和妻子能够花几年时间在欧洲游历，为他们正在密歇根湖畔建造的一座大房子收集古董。在他们离开期间，CDO 成了华尔街炙手可热的新宠。2005 年售出的 CDO 数量创下了历史纪录，打破了 2004 年创下的前纪录。但当 Alec Litowitz 回来并于 2005 年底创办他的新对冲基金时，CDO 世界的情况开始发生变化。

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:48.090
<v Alex Blumberg>We talked to a guy named Bill Tomljanovic a guy on Wall Street who put together CDOs. He said that at the end of 2005, people on Wall Street were starting to worry about the housing market. Unfortunately, since Bill is in finance, he doesn't say it like that. He says it like this--
<v Alex Blumberg>我们采访了一个叫 Bill Tomljanovic 的人，他是华尔街从事 CDO 业务的从业者。他说，2005 年底，华尔街的人们开始担心住房市场。不幸的是，因为 Bill 是搞金融的，他没这么说。他是这样说的——

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:54.690
<v Bill Tomljanovic>In 2005, the summer, spreads on RMBS collateral started expanding.
<v Bill Tomljanovic>2005 年夏天，住宅抵押贷款支持证券（RMBS）的利差开始扩大。

00:07:54.690 --> 00:08:00.580
<v Jesse Eisinger>And that is banker-speak for people started to worry about the housing market?
<v Jesse Eisinger>那是银行家的黑话，意思是人们开始担心住房市场了吗？

00:08:00.580 --> 00:08:01.930
<v Bill Tomljanovic>Yes, ah yes.
<v Bill Tomljanovic>是的，啊，是的。

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:33.350
<v Alex Blumberg>Now what Bill is saying here, which sounds really boring, it is huge. It means that part of our theory, the theory that got us into this story in the first place, was correct. There was a group of people on Wall Street who are not like most people in America. They were not caught by surprise. In 2005, two full years before the collapse of the major subprime lenders like New Century and Countrywide, three full years before the failure of Lehman Brothers and AIG and the big government bailout, a lot of people on Wall Street were looking at housing prices around the country and seeing signs that things were not well.
<v Alex Blumberg>Bill 在这里所说的，听起来非常枯燥，但这意义重大。这意味着我们的理论，也就是让我们最初深入这个故事的理论，是正确的。华尔街有一群人与美国的大多数人不同。他们并没有被突如其来的崩溃吓到。2005 年，也就是 New Century 和 Countrywide 等主要次贷机构倒闭前整整两年，雷曼兄弟、AIG 和政府大规模救助发生前整整三年，华尔街的许多人就已经在观察全国各地的房价，并看到了局势不妙的迹象。

00:08:33.350 --> 00:08:49.380
<v Bill Tomljanovic>Las Vegas had an overheated market. Certain sectors of California were looking very aggressive in terms of real estate valuation. Was it enough-- Was the bubble ready to burst?
<v Bill Tomljanovic>拉斯维加斯市场过热。加州的某些区域在房地产估值方面看起来非常激进。这足够了吗？泡沫准备好破裂了吗？

00:08:49.380 --> 00:09:23.360
<v Alex Blumberg>You could see this uncertainty showing up in bond prices in 2005, long before everything crashed. The anxiety is right there in the interest rate numbers. Some of the people on Wall Street who buy bonds that are made up of hundreds of home mortgages were starting to demand more interest for their investment, saying essentially, we'll buy this bond, but you have to pay us more because it's looking riskier to us. Which is just another way of saying, we're worried. And because of this, people like Bill Tomljanovic were thinking that the good times might be coming to an end in the CDO world.
<v Alex Blumberg>你可以在 2005 年的债券价格中看到这种不确定性，远在一切崩溃之前。这种焦虑直接体现在利率数字上。华尔街一些购买由数百笔住房抵押贷款组成的债券的人开始要求更高的投资回报，本质上是在说：我们会买这种债券，但你必须付给我们更多钱，因为在我们看来它看起来风险更高了。这只是换个说法，意思是我们很担心。正因为如此，像 Bill Tomljanovic 这样的人认为 CDO 世界的好日子可能要到头了。

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:41.870
<v Bill Tomljanovic>The level of difficulty to replicate the business plan of '05 in '06 was pretty-- I mean, everyone was worried, how are we going to keep volume up, make the same amount of money for the firm? How are we going to recreate this in '06 because it was such a great year in '05.
<v Bill Tomljanovic>要在 2006 年复制 2005 年的商业计划的难度相当……我的意思是，每个人都很担心，我们该如何保持交易量，为公司赚取同样多的钱？我们该如何能在 2006 年重现这一点，因为 2005 年是如此美好的一年。

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:52.660
<v Alex Blumberg>So here's all these people on Wall Street in late 2005 thinking, the CDO business might be slowing down, housing might be cooling off, things might be returning to normal. And then in walks Magnetar.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以，2005 年底，华尔街所有这些人都认为 CDO 业务可能会放缓，房地产可能会降温，一切可能会回归正常。然后，Magnetar 入场了。

00:09:52.660 --> 00:10:11.570
<v Jake Bernstein>Magnetar spends the first quarter of '06 talking to as many people as possible in the CDO business, really understanding it, interviewing people, getting a good sort of lay of the land. And then, they put out the word that they want to buy equity.
<v Jake Bernstein>Magnetar 花了 2006 年第一季度的时间，与 CDO 行业内尽可能多的人交谈，真正理解这个行业，采访相关人员，理清局势。然后，他们放出口风，说他们想要购买权益。

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:36.810
<v Alex Blumberg>OK. Equity. A quick word of explanation. Every CDO was divided into layers. A layer at the top that was considered the safest, the layers below that, which were considered semi-safe, and then the bottom layer, which was considered the riskiest. Of course, after the crash, we learned that no part of the CDO was safe. Every part of most subprime CDOs is now worthless. But back in 2005 and 2006, they were still making these distinctions.
<v Alex Blumberg>好的，权益。简单解释一下。每个 CDO 都分为若干层。最顶层被认为是风险最低的，下面是半安全的层级，最底层被认为是风险最高的。当然，崩溃后我们才知道，CDO 的任何部分都不安全。大多数次贷 CDO 的各个部分现在都已经一文不值了。但在 2005 年和 2006 年，他们还在做这些区分。

00:10:36.810 --> 00:11:04.450
<v Alex Blumberg>So the part at the bottom, that riskiest part, that was called the equity tranche. Tranche is just Wall Street lingo for layer. The equity tranche, the equity layer paid a lot more interest than the top layers, the idea being, if you bought that risky equity, you got paid for that risk. But still, it was the riskiest part of a CDO that was made up of risky bonds backed by risky mortgages. It was not an easy sell. Here's Bill Tomljanovic.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以最底部的那部分，也就是风险最高的那部分，被称为权益层（equity tranche）。Tranche 是华尔街对“层”的行话。权益层支付的利息比顶层高得多，意思就是，如果你买了那部分高风险权益，你就会因为承担这种风险而得到报酬。但无论如何，它是 CDO 中风险最高的部分，而该 CDO 本身由以高风险抵押贷款为后盾的高风险债券组成。这并不容易卖出去。这是 Bill Tomljanovic 的说法。

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:12.470
<v Bill Tomljanovic>The equity was the toughest part when the business-- early 2000s either analyzing--
<v Bill Tomljanovic>当业务处于……2000 年代早期分析时，权益是最难的部分……

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:13.990
<v Jesse Eisinger>Finding somebody to take the riskiest portion.
<v Jesse Eisinger>寻找愿意承担最高风险部分的人。

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:15.120
<v Bill Tomljanovic>Riskiest portion.
<v Bill Tomljanovic>最高风险部分。

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:16.320
<v Jesse Eisinger>That was the hardest part to find?
<v Jesse Eisinger>那是寻找起来最难的部分吗？

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:18.570
<v Bill Tomljanovic>It was very difficult, yes.
<v Bill Tomljanovic>是的，非常困难。

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:28.700
<v Alex Blumberg>So here we are in early 2006. CDO managers and investment banks are thinking the CDO party is ending. And in comes this hedge fund saying not only do they want to buy CDOs, but--
<v Alex Blumberg>所以现在是 2006 年初。CDO 经理和投资银行认为 CDO 派对即将结束。这时，这家对冲基金进场了，不仅说他们想买 CDO，而且——

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:34.220
<v Jake Bernstein>They want to buy that lowest tranche, that lowest piece of the CDO.
<v Jake Bernstein>他们想买最低的那一层，CDO 中最低的那一部分。

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:35.400
<v Alex Blumberg>The one that was hardest to place?
<v Alex Blumberg>也就是最难卖出去的那部分？

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:50.040
<v Jake Bernstein>The one that was hardest to place. And they want to buy lots and lots and lots of it. As one banker said to us, it was like a miracle. Suddenly, this big equity buyer shows up.
<v Jake Bernstein>也就是最难卖出去的那部分。而且他们想买很多很多很多。正如一位银行家对我们所说，这就像奇迹一样。突然间，出现了一个巨大的权益买家。

00:11:50.040 --> 00:12:14.040
<v Alex Blumberg>In other words, it seemed like Wall Street felt it had found its sucker. An article in Business Week later wondered if Magnetar was poised to get "shredded." Magnetar's entrance into the CDO market in 2006 had a huge impact though. If they were willing to buy the equity piece, it became easier for Wall Street banks to make the CDO. Buyers were easier to find for the parts that were less risky.
<v Alex Blumberg>换句话说，华尔街似乎觉得它找到了冤大头。《商业周刊》后来甚至猜测 Magnetar 是否准备被“撕碎”。不过，Magnetar 在 2006 年进入 CDO 市场产生了巨大的影响。如果他们愿意购买权益部分，华尔街银行制造 CDO 就变得更容易了。对于风险较低的部分，买家也更容易找到。

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:44.050
<v Alex Blumberg>Magnetar, as the equity buyer, was often called the sponsor of the CDOs in which it bought the equity since, without Magnetar, the CDO probably wouldn't have gotten made in the first place. Magnetar's entry into the market helped the CDO business, which insiders worried was petering out in 2005, roar back to life. 2006 ended up being even bigger than 2005. In fact, it became the best year in CDO history. A lot of CDO deals got done, and a lot of that had to do with Magnetar.
<v Alex Blumberg>作为权益买家，Magnetar 通常被称为其购买权益的 CDO 的“发起人”，因为如果没有 Magnetar，CDO 可能根本就不会被制造出来。Magnetar 进入市场帮助了 CDO 业务，该业务原本在 2005 年被业内人士担心正在消亡，结果却轰轰烈烈地复活了。2006 年最终比 2005 年还要大。事实上，它成了 CDO 历史上最好的一年。大量的 CDO 交易得以达成，而这很大程度上与 Magnetar 有关。

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:57.850
<v Jake Bernstein>Their pace of production was extraordinary by any estimation. In a year's time, from the middle of '06 to the middle of '07, they do 28 deals worth an estimated $40 billion.
<v Jake Bernstein>按照任何估计，他们的生产速度都是非同寻常的。在一年时间里，从 06 年年中到 07 年年中，他们做了 28 笔交易，估计价值 400 亿美元。

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:04.010
<v Alex Blumberg>Just to put that in perspective, that's about a third to a half of this corner of the subprime CDO market that they were operating in.
<v Alex Blumberg>为了说明这一点，这大约占他们所经营的次贷 CDO 市场这个角落的三分之一到二分之一。

00:13:04.010 --> 00:13:08.280
<v Jesse Eisinger>And for one hedge fund to account for that much was very rare.
<v Jesse Eisinger>对于一个对冲基金来说，占据这么大的份额是非常罕见的。

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:21.580
<v Alex Blumberg>In fact, we ran this by Bill Tomljanovic, the CDO manager. He was familiar with Magnetar, but he didn't know how many deals they'd been a part of. And when we ran that number by him, he was pretty impressed.
<v Alex Blumberg>事实上，我们把这个数字告诉了 CDO 经理 Bill Tomljanovic。他熟悉 Magnetar，但他不知道他们参与了多少笔交易。当我们告诉他这个数字时，他非常震惊。

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:24.520
<v Bill Tomljanovic>That's a lot of deals. That's a lot of deals.
<v Bill Tomljanovic>那是很多交易。那是很多交易。

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:28.310
<v Jake Bernstein>Is that enough to sort of be an influence in the market?
<v Jake Bernstein>这足以在市场上产生影响了吗？

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:36.120
<v Bill Tomljanovic>I would say that-- wow-- it sounds big.
<v Bill Tomljanovic>我会说……哇……这听起来很大。

00:13:36.120 --> 00:14:01.120
<v Alex Blumberg>Now most insiders who knew Magnetar would not have described them as suckers. In fact, every single person that Jake and Jesse and I have talked to about Magnetar uses the same word to describe them, smart. Sometimes they'll say, really smart. And yet, here they were going on this huge spending spree, buying the riskiest parts of these financial products that were all backed by a housing market insiders were starting to view with more and more suspicion. And that caused a lot of people to wonder, why?
<v Alex Blumberg>现在，大多数认识 Magnetar 的内部人士不会把他们描述为冤大头。事实上，Jake、Jesse 和我谈过的每一个人在提到 Magnetar 时都用同一个词来形容他们：聪明。有时他们会说，非常聪明。然而，他们却在这场巨大的消费狂潮中，购买这些金融产品中风险最高的部分，而这些产品都是由住房市场支持的，而内部人士正开始对该市场持越来越多的怀疑态度。这让许多人想知道，为什么？

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:27.680
<v Jake Bernstein>People puzzled it out. And they talked about it. And they tried to figure out, well, what's the strategy here? Why would they like such risky stuff? And then they figured it out. As one CDO manager said to us, "At a certain point, we were in on the joke." The joke being that Magnetar was also betting against the very CDOs that it was creating.
<v Jake Bernstein>人们苦思冥想。他们谈论它。他们试图弄明白，好吧，这里的策略是什么？为什么他们会喜欢这么高风险的东西？然后他们想通了。正如一位 CDO 经理对我们所说：“在某个时刻，我们成了这个笑话的一部分。”笑话的内容是，Magnetar 实际上在做空它自己正在制造的 CDO。

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:39.810
<v Alex Blumberg>So how do you bet against a CDO? You don't need to go to Vegas. You don't even need to leave Wall Street. There's another type of financial product that Wall Street had created. It's called a credit default swap. You may have heard that term before as well.
<v Alex Blumberg>那么你如何做空一个 CDO 呢？你不需要去拉斯维加斯。你甚至不需要离开华尔街。华尔街创造了另一种金融产品，叫作信用违约掉期（Credit Default Swap）。你可能也听说过这个词。

00:14:39.810 --> 00:15:04.780
<v Alex Blumberg>A credit default swap can be used to bet that some part of a CDO will fail. And the way it works, you pick a CDO that you think might fail, and then you pay some Wall Street firm a little bit of money a few times a year. If the CDO does fine, the Wall Street firm keeps your money. But if it fails, the Wall Street firm pays you the entire value of the part of the CDO that you're betting against, a massive amount of money.
<v Alex Blumberg>信用违约掉期可以用来赌 CDO 的某一部分会失败。它的运作方式是：你挑选一个你认为可能会失败的 CDO，然后每年支付给某家华尔街公司一点小钱。如果 CDO 表现良好，华尔街公司就保留你的钱。但如果它失败了，华尔街公司就会支付给你你所押注的那部分 CDO 的全部价值，这是一笔巨额资金。

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:35.070
<v Alex Blumberg>The theory on the street was Magnetar didn't care in the long term whether the equity it was buying survived. They made the equity investment mainly as a catalyst for the creation of the rest of the CDO. Magnetar would cause these CDOs to be created by buying this tiny equity portion, which was typically just 5% or 6% of the value of the entire CDO. And then, they'd place a bet, take out a credit default swap on one of the larger sections above. They also placed bets against other, similar CDOs out there.
<v Alex Blumberg>华尔街的理论是，Magnetar 根本不在乎它购买的权益在长期内是否存活。他们进行权益投资主要就是为了推动其余 CDO 的创造。Magnetar 会通过购买这种微小的权益部分（通常仅占整个 CDO 价值的 5% 或 6%）来促使这些 CDO 被创造出来。然后，他们会在上方较大的部分中进行押注，购买信用违约掉期。他们也押注于其他类似的 CDO。

00:15:35.070 --> 00:16:04.460
<v Alex Blumberg>So the theory among many of the people Jake and Jesse interviewed-- and I should emphasize, Magnetar disputes this theory-- but the theory was, say the equity portion cost Magnetar $10 million to buy, but the entire CDO itself is a hundred times that, $1 billion or even $2 billion. If one of the upper sections of the CDO goes bust, and Magnetar is betting against it, they stood to make a lot more than $10 million, potentially hundreds of millions of dollars. It was such a brilliant idea, soon others got in on the act.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以，在 Jake 和 Jesse 采访的许多人中，理论是——我应该强调，Magnetar 反驳这一理论——理论是：假设权益部分花费了 Magnetar 1000 万美元，但整个 CDO 本身价值是其百倍，即 10 亿甚至 20 亿美元。如果 CDO 的上方部分破产了，而 Magnetar 押注了它，他们赚到的钱将远超 1000 万美元，潜在收益可达数亿美元。这是一个如此天才的想法，很快其他人也加入了行动。

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:33.220
<v Jesse Eisinger>This strategy was being employed by some hedge funds, but Magnetar was the biggest. They did it the most. They put the most money toward it. And in fact, on Wall Street, it became known-- according to the people we've talked to-- as the Magnetar trade.
<v Jesse Eisinger>这一策略被一些对冲基金采用，但 Magnetar 是规模最大的。他们做得最多。他们投入了最多的资金。事实上，据我们交谈过的人说，这在华尔街被称为“Magnetar 交易”（The Magnetar trade）。

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:52.780
<v Alex Blumberg>OK. Here's another piece of jargon. The strategy of betting that something will go down in value, in the language of Wall Street, it's called shorting or taking a short position. And there were a handful of other hedge funds out there shorting housing-related securities like CDOs. A new book by author Michael Lewis, The Big Short, tells several of their stories.
<v Alex Blumberg>好的，这里还有另一个术语。赌某样东西价值会下跌的策略，在华尔街的语言中，叫作“做空”或“持空头头寸”。在华尔街，有少数几家对冲基金在做空与房地产相关的证券，比如 CDO。作者 Michael Lewis 的新书《大空头》（The Big Short）讲述了他们的几个故事。

00:16:52.780 --> 00:17:18.530
<v Alex Blumberg>And generally, there's nothing wrong with shorting. In fact, many people argue that shorting, which happens millions and millions of times a day, every single day on Wall Street, is actually a good thing because it keeps prices more tethered to reality. It's harder for a mania to develop if people who have a dim view of things can bet on that view. But the Magnetar trade was not shorting in the traditional sense, betting against something which already exists.
<v Alex Blumberg>总的来说，做空并没有什么问题。事实上，许多人认为做空（华尔街每天发生数百万次）实际上是一件好事，因为它使价格更贴近现实。如果那些对事物持有负面看法的人能够根据自己的看法进行押注，泡沫就更难形成。但 Magnetar 交易并非传统意义上的做空，即赌已经存在的东西会失败。

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:53.010
<v Alex Blumberg>The Magnetar trade was betting against something Magnetar itself helped create, which is the exact opposite of what you want shorting to do. In traditional shorting, by betting, essentially, that certain things out there are crappy and overvalued, you are helping to rid the world of those crappy, overvalued things. But a lot of people thought that what Magnetar was doing was bringing crappy, overvalued things into the world in order to bet against them. We talked to Jon Pickhardt, a lawyer whose firm is suing an investment bank that helped set up some of the deals with Magnetar and with other hedge funds that were doing what Magnetar was doing.
<v Alex Blumberg>Magnetar 交易是押注于 Magnetar 自己参与创造的东西，这与你想要的做空作用完全相反。在传统做空中，通过押注某些东西很烂且被高估，你实际上是在帮助世界摆脱那些烂的、被高估的东西。但许多人认为，Magnetar 所做的是把烂的、被高估的东西带到世界上，以便押注它们。我们采访了律师 Jon Pickhardt，他的公司正在起诉一家投资银行，该银行帮助 Magnetar 和其他采取类似策略的对冲基金建立了一些交易。

00:17:53.010 --> 00:18:04.230
<v Jon Pickhardt>Yeah, a number of the hedge funds, they were simultaneously entering into significant short positions with respect to the CDO or its collateral.
<v Jon Pickhardt>是的，许多对冲基金在就 CDO 或其抵押品持有重大空头头寸。

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:09.720
<v Jesse Eisinger>In other words, were they being basically built to fail?
<v Jesse Eisinger>换句话说，它们基本上是为了失败而建立的？

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:12.910
<v Jon Pickhardt>In some cases, yes.
<v Jon Pickhardt>在某些情况下，是的。

00:18:12.910 --> 00:18:22.360
<v Jesse Eisinger>In trying to come up with an accurate metaphor, have you ever seen The Producers ?
<v Jesse Eisinger>为了找到一个准确的比喻，你看过《制片人》（The Producers）吗？

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:24.060
<v Jon Pickhardt>Yes.
<v Jon Pickhardt>看过。

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:34.320
<v Jesse Eisinger>So in the plot of The Producers, there's a scene where he hires an accountant, and he says, where he's sort of murmuring to himself, and he's like, hm, you could almost make more money if the play fails than if it succeeds.
<v Jesse Eisinger>那么在《制片人》的剧情中，有一个场景是他雇了一名会计，他在嘟囔着什么，他说：嗯，如果剧失败了，你赚的钱可能比它成功时还要多。

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:47.310
<v Leo Bloom>Carry the 3, divided by 4-- amazing! It's absolutely amazing, but under the right circumstances, a producer could make more money with a flop than he could with a hit.
<v Leo Bloom>进位 3，除以 4……太神奇了！这真是太神奇了，但在适当的情况下，制片人赚翻车的钱可能比赚热门剧的钱更多。

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:49.630
<v Jesse Eisinger>It feels very similar. Is it?
<v Jesse Eisinger>感觉非常相似。是这样吗？

00:18:49.630 --> 00:19:01.200
<v Jon Pickhardt>Well, from the hedge fund standpoint, that clearly was the case. Clearly, they would benefit more if the CDO ultimately failed.
<v Jon Pickhardt>嗯，从对冲基金的角度来看，情况确实如此。很显然，如果 CDO 最终失败，他们会受益更多。

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:13.340
<v Max Bialystock>Don't you see Bloom? Darling Bloom, glorious Bloom. It's so simple. Step one, we find the  worst play ever written. Step two, we hire the worst director in town. Step three, I raise $2 million.
<v Max Bialystock>你不明白吗，Bloom？亲爱的 Bloom，光荣的 Bloom。这太简单了。第一步，我们找到史上最烂的剧本。第二步，我们雇佣镇上最烂的导演。第三步，我筹集 200 万美元。

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:13.820
<v Leo Bloom>Two?
<v Leo Bloom>两百万？

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:25.140
<v Max Bialystock>Yes, one for me, one for you. Step four, we hire the worst actors in New York and open on Broadway. And before you can say step  five, we close on Broadway, take our $2 million and go to Rio.
<v Max Bialystock>是的，一份给我，一份给你。第四步，我们雇佣纽约最烂的演员，在百老汇首演。在你还没来得及说第五步之前，我们就在百老汇谢幕，带着我们的 200 万美元去里约热内卢。

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:27.600
<v Leo Bloom>Oh, Rio? Nah, that would never work.
<v Leo Bloom>哦，里约？不，那永远不会成功。

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:30.520
<v Max Bialystock>Oh, ye of little faith.
<v Max Bialystock>哦，你这信心不足的人。

00:19:30.520 --> 00:19:48.290
<v Alex Blumberg>Jake and Jesse have had several conversations with Magnetar, and they say, what they were doing was nothing like what Nathan Lane is singing about here. Magnetar says, "We didn't have an opinion on whether the housing market would crash or soar. We weren't hoping the CDOs would fail. We weren't betting against them. We were simply hedging ourselves."
<v Alex Blumberg>Jake 和 Jesse 与 Magnetar 进行过多次交谈，他们说，他们的所作所为与 Nathan Lane 唱的完全不同。Magnetar 说：“对于房地产市场是会崩溃还是飙升，我们没有立场。我们不是在希望 CDO 失败。我们没有押注它们。我们只是在做对冲。”

00:19:48.290 --> 00:20:17.890
<v Alex Blumberg>Now this is something you hear a lot on Wall Street, the great hedge versus bet debate. A hedge is like insurance. If you're making an investment in something like a house, you expect the house to stay standing, you expect to live in it, but something could happen. It could catch fire or get swept away by a tornado or fall into a sinkhole. So you buy insurance. In other words, you hedge. You pay a little bit a year in case those things happen. But that doesn't mean you're betting that they will.
<v Alex Blumberg>这是你在华尔街经常听到的话：对冲与赌博的大辩论。对冲就像保险。如果你在房子之类的地方进行投资，你会期望房子保持屹立，期望住在里面，但可能会发生什么事。它可能会着火，或被龙卷风扫走，或掉进沉洞。所以你购买保险。换句话说，你对冲。你每年付一点钱以防万一。但这并不意味着你是在赌它一定会发生。

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:46.940
<v Alex Blumberg>And in fact, hedge funds originally got their name because, in the early days of hedge funds, they hedged all kinds of bets. And that is exactly what Magnetar said it was doing, being a classic hedge fund. By buying credit defaults swaps on the CDOs it was helping create, it was simply protecting itself if something unforeseen happened to them. The problem with figuring out whether it's a bet or a hedge, is that the action of betting and the action of hedging look exactly the same. The only difference is intent.
<v Alex Blumberg>事实上，对冲基金之所以得名，是因为在早期的对冲基金中，他们对冲各种赌注。这正是 Magnetar 所说的它在做的事情，即作为一个经典的对冲基金。通过购买它帮助创造的 CDO 的信用违约掉期，如果它们发生不可预见的情况，它只是在保护自己。搞清楚这是赌博还是对冲的问题在于，赌博的行为和对冲的行为看起来完全一样。唯一的区别在于意图。

00:20:46.940 --> 00:21:16.100
<v Alex Blumberg>But there are some clues to look for that help figure out intent. For example, is the size of the bet against the investment bigger than the investment itself? Are they taking out a million dollar insurance policy on a hundred thousand dollar house? So in Magnetar's case, it would be interesting to see how many bets did they make on which CDOs for how much money. Unfortunately, none of this information is public. Still in the case of the Magnetar deals, there is some evidence that the play was expected to flop.
<v Alex Blumberg>但有一些线索可以帮助找出意图。例如，押注于投资的金额是否大于投资本身？他们是在为一栋 10 万美元的房子购买价值 100 万美元的保险单吗？所以，在 Magnetar 的案例中，看看他们对哪些 CDO 押了多少钱是很有趣的。不幸的是，这些信息都不是公开的。尽管如此，在 Magnetar 的交易案例中，确实有一些证据表明该项目被预期会失败。

00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:42.560
<v Alex Blumberg>To understand this evidence, you have to know a little bit about how a CDO gets marketed and sold. OK. So first, you have the investors. This can be anyone who wanted buy a layer of a CDO, could be a hedge fund like Magnetar, a pension fund, an insurance company. They're the buyer. Then you have the seller. That was the Wall Street bank. They were the ones who went out and found the investors, said, "Hey, we're putting together this CDO. Would you like to buy a piece of it?"
<v Alex Blumberg>要理解这些证据，你必须了解一点 CDO 是如何营销和销售的。好的。首先，你有投资者。这可以是任何想购买 CDO 一层的人，可以是像 Magnetar 这样的对冲基金、养老基金、保险公司。他们是买方。然后你有卖方。那是华尔街银行。他们是那些走出去寻找投资者的人，说：“嘿，我们正在组建这个 CDO。你想买一块吗？”

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:56.610
<v Alex Blumberg>And in the CDO business, there was often a third party, the CDO manager. The CDO manager is the one person in this transaction who has what's called fiduciary duty. They're there to make sure that investors aren't being ripped off, that the game is played honestly.
<v Alex Blumberg>在 CDO 业务中，经常有第三方，CDO 经理。CDO 经理是交易中唯一拥有所谓“受托责任”（fiduciary duty）的人。他们在那里的目的是确保投资者不会被敲诈，确保游戏公平进行。

00:21:56.610 --> 00:22:06.870
<v Jake Bernstein>The CDO manager was sort of like the referee. He would be the entity that stood between the investors and the bank.
<v Jake Bernstein>CDO 经理有点像裁判。他将是站在投资者和银行之间的实体。

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:33.600
<v Alex Blumberg>The CDO manager had one other very important function. The CDO manager picked the assets that went into the CDO. OK. Assets. Remember, CDOs are a bunch of mortgage-backed loans bunched together. These mortgage-backed loans, in Wall Street parlance, were called the CDO's assets. They were also called the collateral. It's basically the stuff that goes in to making the CDOs. The CDO manager picked that stuff. Again, here's Jake and Jesse.
<v Alex Blumberg>CDO 经理还有另一个非常重要的职能。CDO 经理选择了进入 CDO 的资产。好的，资产。记住，CDO 是一堆抵押贷款捆绑在一起。这些抵押贷款，用华尔街的行话来说，被称为 CDO 的“资产”。它们也被称为抵押品。它基本上是组建 CDO 的东西。CDO 经理选择了这些东西。再说一次，这是 Jake 和 Jesse 的话。

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:44.650
