00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:39.130
<v Alex Blumberg>And yet, this information wasn't in any of the documents that were available to the Lutherans in Minnesota or the banks in Ohio or the mutual funds in Tennessee or any of the other investors out there, who were buying the other parts of the CDOs that Magnetar was sponsoring. When you look at it this way, if the investment banks and CDO managers had been doing their jobs, actually explaining to their investors what was behind these CDOs, well, the Magnetar trade might not have been possible.
<v Alex Blumberg>然而，这些信息在提供给明尼苏达州的路德教会、俄亥俄州的银行、田纳西州的共同基金，或任何其他正在购买 Magnetar 发起的 CDO 其他部分的外部投资者的任何文件中都没有出现。从这个角度来看，如果投资银行和 CDO 经理尽职尽责，真正向投资者解释了这些 CDO 背后的内容，那么，Magnetar 交易可能就不会发生。

00:32:39.130 --> 00:33:03.220
<v Jesse Eisinger>So if the investment bank came to an investor and said, we have got an investment for you. A hedge fund actually asked us to create it, and they asked us to put riskier assets into the deal, and, oh, by the way, they're betting against it. Would you like to buy it? And the answer is going to be obvious. They're not going to do it.
<v Jesse Eisinger>所以如果投资银行来找投资者说：我们为你准备了一项投资。一家对冲基金实际上要求我们创建它，他们要求我们放入更高风险的资产，而且顺便说一句，他们正在做空它。你想买吗？答案是不言而喻的。他们不会那样做。

00:33:03.220 --> 00:33:38.880
<v Jake Bernstein>The role of Magnetar, both as equity investor and in their bets against the very CDOs they helped create, were not disclosed in any way to investors in the written documents about the deals. Not the marketing material, not the prospectuses, not in the hundreds of pages that an investor could get to see information about the deal was it disclosed that it was, in fact, Magnetar who had helped create the deal and who bet against it.
<v Jake Bernstein>Magnetar 作为权益投资者及其对他们帮助创造的 CDO 的押注，在关于该交易的书面文件中没有任何披露。不在营销材料中，不在招股说明书中，在投资者可以看到关于交易信息的数百页文件中，也没有披露事实上是 Magnetar 帮助创建了该交易并对其进行了押注。

00:33:38.880 --> 00:33:52.890
<v Alex Blumberg>There is an argument to be made that the fact that investment banks and CDO managers weren't disclosing this information might present securities law violations. It might be worth looking into anyway, if you were, say, I don't know, the Securities and Exchange Commission.
<v Alex Blumberg>有一种观点认为，投资银行和 CDO 经理没有披露这一事实，可能违反了证券法。如果你是，比如说，证券交易委员会（SEC），这可能无论如何都值得调查一下。

00:33:52.890 --> 00:34:15.100
<v Jesse Eisinger>Did the banks represent this thing that they're selling fairly and accurately and disclose or tell the investors all the material information? And the question revolves around this concept in the securities law of materiality, which is extremely difficult to define.
<v Jesse Eisinger>银行是否公平、准确地代表了他们正在销售的东西，并披露或告知了投资者所有重大信息？这个问题围绕着证券法中“重大性”（materiality）的概念，这极其难以定义。

00:34:15.100 --> 00:34:24.730
<v Alex Blumberg>And the materiality issue is just basically what is material, what do the investors need to know, and what do they not need to know basically? Like what is material for them to know?
<v Alex Blumberg>关于重大性的问题基本上就是：什么是重大的？投资者需要知道什么，他们不需要知道什么？基本上，什么是他们需要知道的重大事项？

00:34:24.730 --> 00:34:25.190
<v Jesse Eisinger>Exactly.
<v Jesse Eisinger>没错。

00:34:25.190 --> 00:34:29.200
<v Jake Bernstein>Right. To make their decision on whether they want to invest or not.
<v Jake Bernstein>对。为了让他们决定是否要投资。

00:34:29.200 --> 00:35:00.060
<v Alex Blumberg>In the conversations that you've had with the people on Wall Street and the people in the CDO managers and the people from Magnetar and everybody who you've talked to on the inside, was there ever outrage expressed? Did anybody ever say to you, man, what those guys were doing, that was questionable, or that seemed wrong, or that seemed ethically dubious, or anything like that?
<v Alex Blumberg>在你们与华尔街人士、CDO 经理、Magnetar 人员以及你们交谈过的所有内部人员的对话中，有没有人表达过愤怒？有没有人对你说：伙计，那些家伙做的事太可疑了，或者看起来不对，或者看起来在道德上是有问题的？

00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:31.730
<v Jesse Eisinger>The answer is yes. But I think within Wall Street, there's a sense that Magnetar was a predator, a shark, if you will, and that you don't blame the predator for hunting the prey. That's what predators do. But I recall one banker, who we spoke to, who said, "When Magnetar arrived on the scene, we all should have gone running. We all should have taken off."
<v Jesse Eisinger>答案是肯定的。但我认为在华尔街内部，有一种感觉是 Magnetar 是掠食者，如果你愿意的话，就是鲨鱼，你不能责怪掠食者去捕猎猎物。这就是掠食者做的事。但我记得我们采访过的一位银行家说：“当 Magnetar 出现时，我们所有人都应该逃跑。我们所有人都应该离开。”

00:35:31.730 --> 00:35:39.070
<v Jake Bernstein>Yeah, yeah, he said, "We should have run for the hills, everyone, all of us in America."
<v Jake Bernstein>是的，是的，他说：“我们都应该逃到山里去，每个人，我们美国所有人。”

00:35:39.070 --> 00:35:43.320
<v Alex Blumberg>And what did he see as the problem?
<v Alex Blumberg>他认为问题是什么？

00:35:43.320 --> 00:36:11.180
<v Jesse Eisinger>What he saw was that Magnetar had figured out how dysfunctional the system had become and was going to exploit that dysfunction. And that it should have been a sign to everybody that there was something wrong. There was something wrong with the way Wall Street was operating.
<v Jesse Eisinger>他所看到的是，Magnetar 已经弄清楚这个系统变得多么功能失调，并准备利用这种功能失调。这应该给所有人敲响警钟：出了问题。华尔街的运作方式出了问题。

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:36.330
<v Alex Blumberg>Even if what was happening here wasn't illegal, it had profound consequences for the financial system, the taxpayers, and the global economy. If the CDO market had been allowed to cool off at the end of 2005, as market insiders thought it might, the financial crisis almost certainly would not have been as bad as it was. Magnetar, by entering the market when it did, by catalyzing the volume of production that it did, extended the mania and exacerbated the crash.
<v Alex Blumberg>即使这里发生的事情并不违法，它也对金融体系、纳税人和全球经济产生了深远的影响。如果 CDO 市场在 2005 年底被允许降温（正如市场内部人士所预期的那样），那么金融危机几乎肯定不会像现在这样糟糕。Magnetar 通过在那个时候进入市场，通过催化其所实现的生产规模，延长了狂热并加剧了崩溃。

00:36:36.330 --> 00:37:02.810
<v Alex Blumberg>Of the 24 Magnetar CDOs that ProPublica was able to track down, 23 of them are nearly worthless today. In total, nearly $40 billion evaporated. A good portion of that $40 billion was held by the banks, no doubt part of the hole the taxpayers had to cover when we bailed out the financial system.
<v Alex Blumberg>在 ProPublica 能够追踪到的 24 个 Magnetar CDO 中，23 个在今天几乎一文不值。总共有近 400 亿美元化为乌有。这 400 亿美元中很大一部分是由银行持有的，毫无疑问，这也是我们在救助金融体系时纳税人必须填补的黑洞的一部分。

00:37:02.810 --> 00:37:34.220
<v Alex Blumberg>The Magnetar trade also had a direct impact on the housing market all over the country. If the CDO business had gone down, as people were predicting in 2005, home mortgages would've been less available in 2006 and 2007 for people buying houses in California and Florida. There would have been less money out there looking for riskier and riskier mortgages to stuff into mortgage-backed securities to feed the CDOs that Magnetar was helping to create. There would have been, in short, fewer people in houses they couldn't afford then and fewer people facing foreclosure today.
<v Alex Blumberg>Magnetar 交易对全国各地的住房市场也产生了直接影响。如果 CDO 业务正如 2005 年人们预测的那样倒闭，那么 2006 年和 2007 年，在加州和佛罗里达州购房的人将更难获得住房抵押贷款。市场上将没有那么多资金寻找风险越来越高的抵押贷款来填充抵押贷款支持证券，从而去喂养 Magnetar 帮助创建的 CDO。简而言之，那时就不会有那么多人在负担不起的房子里，今天面临止赎的人也会更少。

00:37:34.220 --> 00:37:51.750
<v Alex Blumberg>People making short-term decisions for their own short-term profit added to the pain we're all going through now. Fortunes were made on CDO desks all over Wall Street in 2006 and 2007. Some of the banks where those fortunes were made may be gone, but the people who made them and the fortunes themselves remain.
<v Alex Blumberg>为了短期利润而做出短期决策的人加剧了我们现在正在经历的痛苦。2006 年和 2007 年，华尔街各地的 CDO 部门创造了财富。那些创造财富的银行可能已经不复存在，但创造财富的人和财富本身依然存在。

00:37:51.750 --> 00:38:19.100
<v Alex Blumberg>According to a magazine which tracks hedge fund pay, Alec Litowitz, the head of Magnetar, personally took home an estimated $280 million in 2007. Magnetar declined to comment on that figure. Unlike some of the Wall Street banks with whom the fund worked, Magnetar is still open for business and still looking for ways to make its clients money.
<v Alex Blumberg>据一本追踪对冲基金薪酬的杂志报道，Magnetar 的负责人 Alec Litowitz 在 2007 年个人进账估计达 2.8 亿美元。Magnetar 对该数字拒绝置评。与该基金合作过的一些华尔街银行不同，Magnetar 仍在营业，并仍在寻找为其客户赚钱的方法。

00:38:19.100 --> 00:38:46.230
<v Ira Glass>Alex Blumberg, who's part of our Planet Money team. Planet Money is a co-production between our program and NPR News. This story was done as a collaboration with ProPublica and the reporters Jake Bernstein and Jesse Eisinger. A statement from Magnetar and its full response to our questions, emails between a CDO manager and Magnetar, a timeline of the deals, analysis, lots of documents, a more detailed version of this story are all at ProPublica's website propublica.org.
<v Ira Glass>Alex Blumberg，他是我们 Planet Money 团队的成员。Planet Money 是我们节目与 NPR 新闻的联合制作。这个故事是与 ProPublica 合作完成的，报道者是 Jake Bernstein 和 Jesse Eisinger。关于 Magnetar 的一份声明及其对我们问题的全面回复、CDO 经理与 Magnetar 之间的电子邮件、交易时间表、分析、大量文件以及这个故事的更详细版本，都在 ProPublica 的网站 propublica.org 上。

00:38:46.230 --> 00:39:28.880
<v Ira Glass>By the way, just this week, the former CEO of Citigroup, Charles Prince, testified before the financial crisis inquiry commission about CDOs. Citigroup did several CDO deals with Magnetar. The names of those deals, Cetus 2 and 4, Octans 3, Lacerta, each of these deals was over $1 billion. Now they're almost worthless, of course. Charles Prince told the commission that CDOs made up most of the losses at Citigroup. He said, these were the losses, CDOs, that required the government to bail out the bank even though at the time, he said, everyone believed they were totally safe investments. Citi bought the best parts of the CDOs, what Prince called--
<v Ira Glass>顺便提一下，就在本周，花旗集团（Citigroup）前首席执行官 Charles Prince 在金融危机调查委员会作证时谈到了 CDO。花旗集团与 Magnetar 做了几笔 CDO 交易。这些交易的名字分别是 Cetus 2 和 4、Octans 3、Lacerta，每一笔交易的规模都超过 10 亿美元。当然，现在它们几乎一文不值了。Charles Prince 告诉委员会，CDO 造成了花旗集团的大部分损失。他说，正是这些损失，这些 CDO，导致政府必须救助银行，尽管当时他说，每个人都相信它们是完全安全的投资。花旗购买了 CDO 的最佳部分，也就是 Prince 所说的——

00:39:28.880 --> 00:39:51.140
<v Charles Prince>--super senior tranches of CDOs that carried the lowest possible risk of default. It bears emphasis that Citi was by no means alone in this view, and that everyone, including our risk managers, government regulators, other banks, and CDO structurers, all believed that these securities held virtually no risk.
<v Charles Prince>……CDO 的超高级层（super senior tranches），承担了最低的违约风险。值得强调的是，花旗绝对不是唯一持这种观点的人，每个人，包括我们的风险经理、政府监管机构、其他银行和 CDO 构建者，都相信这些证券几乎没有任何风险。

00:39:51.140 --> 00:40:03.450
<v Ira Glass>Prince emphasized that even the Citibank employees who made the decisions to keep these CDOs on the books had no reason to suspect that they might go bad. In other words, no one saw it coming.
<v Ira Glass>Prince 强调，甚至连那些决定将这些 CDO 保留在账面上的花旗银行员工也没有理由怀疑它们可能会变坏。换句话说，没人预见到会发生。

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:03.920
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "BET AGAINST THE AMERICAN DREAM"]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “做空美国梦”（BET AGAINST THE AMERICAN DREAM）]

00:40:03.920 --> 00:41:31.250
<v Broadway-style Singers>Step one, we write a check for $10 million, hand the check to a Wall Street bank and ask them to make us a CDO. Step two, they create the CDO using risky stuff, very risky stuff, extremely risky stuff. Step three, other investors commit hundreds of millions of dollars to the CDO. Step four, we bet against the CDO using a credit default swap. Step five, the housing market crashes, the CDO's value drops to 0, our bet pays off, and we make hundreds of millions of dollars. And before you can say, step six, we're rich. We're gonna bet against the American dream. We're gonna be on the winning team. Purchase risky debt on a massive scale, then place a bet that the debt will fail. Hundreds of millions for Magnetar, the economy collapsing like a dying star. No one will know 'til it's on NPR. And who cares? It's time to hit the town. This sucker could go down. The housing market's losing steam. And all we gotta do to make our dreams come true, is bet against the American dream.
<v Broadway-style Singers>第一步，我们开出一张 1000 万美元的支票，交给一家华尔街银行，让他们为我们做一个 CDO。第二步，他们用高风险的东西创建 CDO，非常高风险的东西，极其高风险的东西。第三步，其他投资者向该 CDO 投入数亿美元。第四步，我们利用信用违约掉期对 CDO 进行做空。第五步，住房市场崩溃，CDO 的价值降为 0，我们的赌注成功，我们赚了数亿美元。在你来得及说第六步之前，我们就富了。我们将做空美国梦。我们将进入获胜的团队。大规模购买高风险债务，然后押注债务会失败。Magnetar 赚取数亿，经济像垂死的恒星一样崩溃。在 NPR 报道之前没人会知道。谁在乎呢？是时候去城里庆祝了。这个冤大头可能会倒下。房地产市场正在失去动力。为了实现我们的梦想，我们所要做的就是做空美国梦。

00:41:31.250 --> 00:42:02.660
<v Ira Glass>John Treacy Eagan and Christian Borle on vocals. "Bet Against The American Dream," the music and lyrics were written for our show by Robert Lopez, produced by music supervisor Steven Oremus. The orchestrator was Bruce Coughlin. To see a video of these amazing Broadway performers recording the song, go to Planet Money's website npr.org/money. Coming up, what cops know about cops. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>John Treacy Eagan 和 Christian Borle 主唱。《做空美国梦》，音乐和歌词是 Robert Lopez 为我们的节目创作的，由音乐主管 Steven Oremus 制作。编曲是 Bruce Coughlin。要观看这些神奇的百老汇表演者录制这首歌的视频，请访问 Planet Money 的网站 npr.org/money。接下来，警察对警察了解些什么。请在一分钟后收听，节目来自芝加哥公共广播（Chicago Public Radio）和公共广播国际（Public Radio International）。

00:42:02.660 --> 00:42:20.430
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you reporting, interviews, songs, anything we can think of on that theme. Today's show, Inside Job. We've arrived at Act Two of our show. Act Two, Taking A Big Pink Eraser To The Thin Blue Line.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是 Ira Glass。当然，每周我们都会在我们的节目中选择一个主题，为您带来关于该主题的报道、采访、歌曲以及我们能想到的任何内容。今天的节目是“监守自盗”。我们已经到了节目的第二幕。第二幕，“用巨大的粉色橡皮擦擦掉那条细细的蓝线”。

00:42:20.430 --> 00:42:35.510
<v Ira Glass>You may have heard of these self-produced DVDs called Never Get Busted Again. In them, there's a guy named Barry Cooper who shows small-time pot growers how to avoid trouble with the police. For example, how to handle yourself when a cop stops you on the highway with his drug-sniffing dog.
<v Ira Glass>你可能听说过这些名为《绝不再被捕》（Never Get Busted Again）的自制 DVD。里面有一个叫 Barry Cooper 的家伙，他向小规模的大麻种植者展示如何避免与警察发生麻烦。例如，当警察带着缉毒犬在高速公路上拦下你时，你该如何应对。

00:42:35.510 --> 00:42:49.050
<v Barry Cooper>It's a good idea to carry a cat in your car if you're going to have a couple of marijuana cigarettes. This confuses the dog, where his drive is channeled to chasing these things instead of looking for the marijuana.
<v Barry Cooper>如果你打算带几支大麻烟卷，在车里带一只猫是个好主意。这会干扰狗，它的驱动力会转而去追逐这些东西，而不是寻找大麻。

00:42:49.050 --> 00:42:59.980
<v Ira Glass>He also explains how to grow pot without getting caught indoors or out, how to spot undercovers and informants, how to handle knock and talks and rap and taps-- that's when a cop tries to talk his way into your house.
<v Ira Glass>他还解释了如何在室内或室外种植大麻而不被抓，如何识别便衣警察和告密者，如何处理警察敲门盘查和轻敲传唤——也就是警察试图通过谈话进入你家的情况。

00:42:59.980 --> 00:43:15.900
<v Barry Cooper>If you hear a knock at your door and, upon investigating, you notice it's the police, if your door is not locked, go ahead and lock it right then. I don't open the door for police.
<v Barry Cooper>如果你听到敲门声，检查后发现是警察，如果你的门没锁，立刻锁上。我不给警察开门。

00:43:15.900 --> 00:43:38.970
<v Ira Glass>That's Barry demonstrating how you should yell through the window at the officer. All this advice could really only come from a consummate insider. And Barry Cooper is one. He's a former narcotics cop and a dirty narcotics cop at that. And recently he has moved on from the DVDs to a new venture, again, drawing on his experience in law enforcement. Michael May tells the story from Austin.
<v Ira Glass>那是 Barry 在演示你应该如何通过窗户对警察大喊。所有这些建议确实只能来自一位彻底的内部人士。Barry Cooper 就是这样的人。他曾是一名缉毒警察，而且还是个脏警察。最近，他从 DVD 转到了一个新的领域，同样是借鉴了他在执法方面的经验。Michael May 在奥斯汀讲述了这个故事。

00:43:38.970 --> 00:43:51.870
<v Michael May>I've watched videos taken from the dashboard camera on Barry Cooper's squad car in the mid-'90s. Back then, he had short, cropped hair, cop mustache. He liked to lean into a suspect with a smirk that said, I know you're lying.
<v Michael May>我看过 90 年代中期 Barry Cooper 警车仪表盘摄像头拍摄的视频。那时，他留着短平头，留着警察胡子。他喜欢倾斜身子对着嫌疑人，脸上带着一种表情，仿佛在说：“我知道你在撒谎。”

00:43:51.870 --> 00:43:53.910
<v Barry Cooper>OK. Where's the marijuana at?
<v Barry Cooper>好的，大麻在哪儿？

00:43:53.910 --> 00:43:54.520
<v Man>I ain't got none.
<v Man>我没有。

00:43:54.520 --> 00:43:56.810
<v Barry Cooper>These two ladies in here been smoking weed too?
<v Barry Cooper>这两个女士也在抽大麻吗？

00:43:56.810 --> 00:43:57.300
<v Man>No.
<v Man>不。

00:43:57.300 --> 00:44:29.800
<v Michael May>Barry was a member of the Permian Basin Drug Task Force, a west Texas unit that became notorious for using unscrupulous tactics in the war on drugs. It was eventually shut down by the FBI in the late '90s. But to Barry, it was a great assignment. He learned all the ways to bend the law to rack up, harass, and chase down suspects. Barry was in his early 20s, and he says, the thing he loved most about being a cop was the adrenaline rush. One of his favorite things to do was to pull people over on the highway, and then, just for kicks, incite a chase. How?
<v Michael May>Barry 是二叠纪盆地（Permian Basin）缉毒特遣部队的成员，这是一个西德克萨斯单位，以在禁毒战争中使用不择手段的策略而闻名。它最终在 90 年代末被 FBI 关闭。但对 Barry 来说，这是一份很棒的差事。他学会了所有歪曲法律、诬陷、骚扰和追捕嫌疑人的方法。Barry 那时才 20 多岁出头，他说，他作为警察最喜欢的就是肾上腺素的冲动。他最喜欢做的事情之一就是在高速公路上拦下人，然后，仅仅是为了好玩，引诱他们逃跑。怎么做？

00:44:29.800 --> 00:45:17.200
<v Barry Cooper>They taught us in the academy that once we found drugs on somebody to handcuff them immediately. Instead of doing that, I would look at the suspect and say, "Hmm, well, some crack cocaine I found in your pocket. You're under arrest for a felony. You're going to go to prison for life." Whether he was or not, he didn't know that. And I would just turn around and walk to the patrol car to get my paperwork ready and to radio it in, giving that suspect time to run. And they often did. And then the foot chase would be on, and then the fight would ensue, and that would get my adrenaline fix.
<v Barry Cooper>他们在学院教我们，一旦我们在某人身上发现毒品，就立即给他们戴上手铐。我不这样做，我会看着嫌疑人说：“嗯，我在你口袋里发现了一些快克可卡因。你因重罪被捕。你要去监狱蹲一辈子。”无论他是否真的会蹲监狱，他自己不知道。然后我就会转过身走向警车准备文书工作并呼叫总部，给嫌疑人逃跑的时间。他们经常这样做。然后步行的追逐就开始了，接着是搏斗，那能让我获得肾上腺素的快感。

00:45:17.200 --> 00:45:55.470
<v Barry Cooper>I was one of the biggest [BLEEP] you'd ever want to meet in a drug deal. A lot of what I was doing was doing illegal searches, such as making my dog false alert or saying, I had an informant to raid a house when I never did. And we would raid a house. It's called using a ghost informant. Or conducting illegal searches on citizens to seize narcotics. It also includes stealing money. I never planted drugs, but I often threatened to to scare citizens into becoming my informant.
<v Barry Cooper>在毒品交易中，我是你能见到的最糟糕的混蛋之一。我做的很多事情都是非法搜查，比如让我的狗发出假警报，或者说我有告密者去突袭一所房子，而我根本没有。我们会突袭一所房子。这叫使用“幽灵告密者”。或者对公民进行非法搜查以查获毒品。这还包括偷钱。我从没栽赃毒品，但我经常威胁要这么做，以恐吓公民成为我的告密者。

00:45:55.470 --> 00:46:30.950
<v Michael May>Barry served eight years on the force, but after getting caught doing an overzealous search of a black man's underwear looking for drugs, his department was sued in a federal civil rights case. They settled, but Barry left the force anyway, frustrated and angry that his superiors didn't defend him. He bounced around for a few years after that, opening several used car lots, founding a church, even starting a cage fighting business. But it was his next step that truly changed his life when he fell in love with his current wife, Candi, and began smoking the substance he'd spent years arresting people for.
<v Michael May>Barry 在警队服役了八年，但在因为搜查一名黑人的内衣搜寻毒品过于狂热被捕后，他的部门在一场联邦民权诉讼中被起诉。他们和解了，但 Barry 还是离开了警队，因为他的上级没有维护他而感到沮丧和愤怒。之后他跳槽了好几年，开了几家二手车行，创立了一所教会，甚至还创办了一家笼斗生意。但真正改变他人生的是他的下一步：他爱上了现在的妻子 Candi，并开始抽起他多年来一直为此逮捕别人的大麻。

00:46:30.950 --> 00:47:34.120
<v Barry Cooper>I spent the next year literally in her bedroom, her and I growing close together, talking and smoking pot. I'd never eaten a pizza in bed in my life until then. We would order pizza and smoke marijuana. And the first thing I did was laugh and laugh and laugh and laugh. And I just couldn't believe the joy I was feeling. That would turn into crying. And she knew I had a lot of guilt. I'd start talking to her about how bad I felt about the stuff I did to people for this marijuana that I was enjoying and that was healing me. So she was smart enough to help me get through those flashbacks. And she'd tell me, "That was rotten what you did, Barry, but you were doing what you thought was right. And the important thing is now, humans can change, and people will forgive you."
<v Barry Cooper>我在她卧室里度过了接下来的一整年，她和我变得亲近，聊天、抽大麻。在此之前，我一生中从未在床上吃过披萨。我们会点披萨，抽大麻。我做的第一件事就是大笑，不停地笑。我简直不敢相信我感到的快乐。那会转变为哭泣。她知道我有很深的罪恶感。我会开始告诉她，对于我为了现在正在享受并治愈我的大麻而对人们做的事情，我感到有多么糟糕。所以她足够聪明，帮我度过了那些闪回。她会告诉我：“Barry，你做的事很烂，但你是在做你认为对的事。重要的是现在，人类是可以改变的，人们会原谅你的。”

00:47:34.120 --> 00:48:07.020
<v Michael May>Barry wanted to atone. So this is when he created the Never Get Busted DVDs. In three years, he says, he sold over 50,000. Today, Barry had long hair, a goatee, hoop earrings, and a pin-up girl tattooed on his neck. He smiles a lot and cries easily and says, "I love you" as a casual greeting, part redneck, part hippie, part ex-cop. When I introduced him to my wife, she told me she felt like she'd just walked into a Coen brothers movie. And Barry's next big idea was like something straight out of one of those movies.
<v Michael May>Barry 想赎罪。所以就在这时，他制作了《绝不再被捕》系列 DVD。他说，三年里他卖了 5 万多套。今天，Barry 留着长发，蓄着山羊胡，戴着圈形耳环，脖子上纹着一个海报女郎。他经常微笑，很容易流泪，并把“我爱你”作为随意的问候语，他部分是红脖子，部分是嬉皮士，部分是前警察。当我把他介绍给我妻子时，她告诉我，她觉得好像走进了一部科恩兄弟的电影。而 Barry 的下一个宏大想法就像直接出自那些电影一样。

00:48:07.020 --> 00:48:53.070
<v Michael May>He decided he wanted to do more than just help potheads. He wanted to expose and punish the cops that put them in prison. Barry says, the war on drugs has warped the priorities of law enforcement. Small-town police fund their own salaries by seizing cash and property in drug raids. Incredibly, they don't even have to charge someone with a crime to keep the stuff. And he says, that's given cops a reason to routinely bend the law to bust people. Which brings us to Barry's latest great idea. It involves chases and traps and suspicious props and anonymous tips and TV. Barry would set up elaborate stings to catch cops in the act of breaking the law. And he'd film it for a reality TV show he wanted to create called KopBusters, spelled with a K.
<v Michael May>他决定他想做的不仅仅是帮助大麻客。他想揭露并惩罚那些把他们送进监狱的警察。Barry 说，禁毒战争已经扭曲了执法的优先事项。小镇警察通过在突袭毒品行动中查获现金和财产来资助他们自己的工资。令人难以置信的是，他们甚至不需要指控某人犯有罪行就能保留这些东西。他说，这给了警察 routinely 歪曲法律以抓捕人的理由。这就引出了 Barry 最新的伟大想法。它涉及追逐、陷阱、可疑的道具、匿名线索和电视。Barry 会设立精心设计的圈套来抓捕正在触犯法律的警察。而且他会为了他想创造的一个叫作“KopBusters”（用 K 开头拼写）的现实电视节目拍下来。

00:48:53.070 --> 00:49:00.440
<v Announcer>Every year, thousands of innocent people are sent to prison, many because of corrupt cops.
<v Announcer>每年，成千上万无辜的人被送进监狱，许多是因为腐败的警察。

00:49:00.440 --> 00:49:02.400
<v Barry Cooper>Do you have a search warrant?
<v Barry Cooper>你有搜查证吗？

00:49:02.400 --> 00:49:26.470
<v Michael May>This is the KopBusters trailer. Barry put it together to shop his idea around to TV networks. And in 2008, Barry decides to test his premise by stinging cops in Odessa, where he once worked on the task force. He believed that cops were corrupt there and had a plan to prove it. And Barry also had a secret weapon, an unlikely benefactor, one with deep pockets.
<v Michael May>这是 KopBusters 的预告片。Barry 把这个整合起来，向电视网推销他的想法。2008 年，Barry 决定通过在 Odessa（他曾在那里的特遣部队工作过）设置陷阱来测试他的前提。他相信那里的警察很腐败，并计划证明这一点。而且 Barry 还有一件秘密武器，一位不太可能的捐赠者，一位财大气粗的捐赠者。

00:49:26.470 --> 00:49:58.450
<v Michael May>His name was Raymond Madden, and he was a conservative middle-aged businessman. For most of his life, Raymond trusted the police and voted tough on crime. Then his daughter, Yolanda, was arrested for having an ounce of meth and sentenced to eight years in prison. Madden was convinced the police had planted the drugs on her. A police informant even testified at her trial that he'd framed her. Madden spent years trying to get activists and reporters interested in the case to no avail. Then he came across Barry Cooper on the internet.
<v Michael May>他的名字叫 Raymond Madden，他是一位保守的中年商人。在他生命的大部分时间里，Raymond 信任警察并对犯罪投下严厉打击的一票。然后他的女儿 Yolanda 被捕，因持有一盎司甲基苯丙胺被判入狱八年。Madden 确信警察在她身上栽赃了毒品。一名警方告密者甚至在她的审判中作证说他陷害了她。Madden 花了几年时间试图让活动家和记者对这个案件感兴趣，但无济于事。然后他在网上遇到了 Barry Cooper。

00:49:58.450 --> 00:50:34.400
<v Raymond Madden>The first thing I got of Barry was his videos. And I thought, "What a nut job this guy is." But I was desperate. So you're talking about a father who's desperate. I mean, I was running out of options. Barry knew a lot of the players in this deal because he had been involved in the Odessa scene. And he had a knowledge that I didn't have. He knew how cops think. I didn't. That's where I always kept making my mistakes is I went up to people thinking they were going to be honest. But he knew how they thought.
<v Raymond Madden>我从 Barry 那里得到的第一样东西是他的视频。我想：“这个家伙是个疯子。”但我绝望了。所以你面对的是一个绝望的父亲。我的意思是，我的选择用完了。Barry 对这笔交易中的许多参与者都很了解，因为他曾经参与过 Odessa 的现场。他拥有我没有的知识。他知道警察是怎么想的。我不知道。这就是我总是不断犯错的地方，我走近人们，认为他们会诚实。但他知道他们是怎么想的。

00:50:34.400 --> 00:51:02.290
<v Raymond Madden>And so I kind of think of Barry as like some of these-- I call them blue-haired biddies of [? Baptist, ?] they're blue-haired-- that used to smoke and don't smoke. My gosh, there's nothing worse than a reformed smoker to be around. You know? Or a reformed drunk. Well, Barry was a reformed cop. And my gosh, there's probably nothing more sanctimonious.
<v Raymond Madden>所以我有点把 Barry 看作是那些——我称他们为浸信会的蓝发老太，他们是蓝头发——曾经抽烟现在不抽了。天哪，没有什么比改邪归正的吸烟者更难相处了。你知道吗？或者一个改邪归正的酒鬼。嗯，Barry 是一个改邪归正的警察。天哪，可能没有什么比这更自以为是的了。

00:51:02.290 --> 00:51:27.800
<v Michael May>Barry told Raymond he couldn't get Yolanda out of prison, but he could embarrass the police and get the press to look into her case. His plan went like this: set up a fake marijuana grow house and get the Odessa police to raid it illegally. Inside, he put a single grow light over a couple of Christmas trees, Barry's idea of a punch line. He'd invite local media along to catch the police looking like fools when they busted in.
<v Michael May>Barry 告诉 Raymond，他无法让 Yolanda 出狱，但他可以使警察尴尬，并让媒体去调查她的案件。他的计划是这样的：设置一个假的室内大麻种植屋，让 Odessa 警察非法突袭它。在里面，他在几棵圣诞树上放了一盏单一的生长灯，这是 Barry 对笑话的构思。他会邀请当地媒体陪同，捕捉警察在闯入时看起来像傻瓜的样子。

00:51:27.800 --> 00:52:09.100
<v Michael May>Raymond spent over $30,000 setting this all up. Barry rented a house, wired it with four cameras, bought laptops to watch the video streaming live, hired a crew and a lawyer and put them all up in hotels while they set the trap. To bait the police, Barry's crew sends an anonymous letter to a local church, promising a house full of pot plants and $19,000 in drug money. An anonymous tip alone is not enough for a search warrant. Barry was hoping they'd search the house illegally, while, of course, he filmed everything. So the letter goes out to the church. Oh yeah, it also mentions the money is going to be gone by the next day. 14 hours later--
<v Michael May>Raymond 花了超过 3 万美元设置这一切。Barry 租了一间房子，安装了四台摄像头，买了笔记本电脑观察直播视频，雇了一个团队和一名律师，并将他们全部安置在酒店里，同时他们设置了这个陷阱。为了引诱警察，Barry 的团队向当地的一家教会发送了一封匿名信，承诺有一屋子大麻植物和 1.9 万美元的毒资。仅凭匿名举报不足以获得搜查证。Barry 希望他们非法搜查房子，当然，他拍摄了一切。所以信发到了教会。哦对了，它还提到钱第二天就会消失。14 小时后——

00:52:09.100 --> 00:52:10.650
<v Barry Cooper>[UNINTELLIGIBLE] Back up. Zoom out. Zoom out.
<v Barry Cooper>[听不清] 后退。缩放。缩放。

00:52:10.650 --> 00:52:11.320
<v Man>And here come the cops.
<v Man>警察来了。

00:52:11.320 --> 00:52:18.640
<v Michael May>Barry and his team are sitting in the hotel room watching the scene on computers when the cops burst in the back door of the house with guns drawn.
<v Michael May>Barry 和他的团队坐在酒店房间里通过电脑观看现场，这时警察拔着枪闯入了房子的后门。

00:52:18.640 --> 00:52:19.580
<v Man>They're in. They're in.
<v Man>他们进来了。他们进来了。

00:52:19.580 --> 00:52:20.410
<v Barry Cooper>They're raiding it.
<v Barry Cooper>他们在突袭。

00:52:20.410 --> 00:52:31.040
<v Michael May>The police walk in the living room and stand in front of a poster Barry has hung on the wall. It tells them they're on KopBusters. They lower their guns. One says, "We've been set up, huh?"
<v Michael May>警察走进客厅，站在 Barry 挂在墙上的海报前。它告诉他们他们正在上 KopBusters。他们放下了枪。一个警察说：“我们被陷害了，哈？”

00:52:31.040 --> 00:52:31.510
<v Barry Cooper>Let's go.
<v Barry Cooper>我们走。

00:52:31.510 --> 00:52:41.790
<v Michael May>Barry jumps in the car to go confront the cops before they leave. He's clearly high on adrenaline at this point just like the old days. He jumps out of his car.
<v Michael May>Barry 跳上车去在警察离开前与他们对峙。他在此时显然像过去一样，处于肾上腺素飙升的状态。他跳下车。

00:52:41.790 --> 00:52:46.020
<v Barry Cooper>Hey, I'm Barry Cooper with KopBusters. Why are you in my house?
<v Barry Cooper>嘿，我是 KopBusters 的 Barry Cooper。你们为什么在我家里？

00:52:46.020 --> 00:52:55.930
<v Michael May>Barry runs into the street wearing a bright red t-shirt with the words "Free Yolanda" printed on it. He starts hollering at the police. They tell him if he doesn't get on the sidewalk, they're going to arrest him.
<v Michael May>Barry 穿着一件印有“释放 Yolanda”字样的亮红色 T 恤跑进街上。他开始对警察大喊大叫。他们告诉他如果他不走到人行道上，他们就要逮捕他。

00:52:55.930 --> 00:52:57.560
<v Policeman>We're not giving nobody no hassle.
<v Policeman>我们没给任何人添麻烦。

00:52:57.560 --> 00:53:02.750
<v Barry Cooper>Y'all planted drugs on Yolanda, and she's in prison because of it. That's giving people a hassle.
<v Barry Cooper>你们在 Yolanda 身上栽赃了毒品，她因为这个在监狱里。那才叫给人们添麻烦。

00:53:02.750 --> 00:53:05.580
<v Policeman>Sir, you've been warned. You've been warned. I'm glad that--
<v Policeman>先生，你已经被警告了。你已经被警告了。我很高兴——

00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:06.550
<v Barry Cooper>You've been warned.
<v Barry Cooper>你已经被警告了。

00:53:06.550 --> 00:53:07.130
<v Policeman>You've been warned.
<v Policeman>你已经被警告了。

00:53:07.130 --> 00:53:07.410
<v Barry Cooper>You've been warned.
<v Barry Cooper>你已经被警告了。

00:53:07.410 --> 00:53:09.480
<v Policeman>You're blocking the roadway. You can go to jail for it.
<v Policeman>你挡住了车道。你可以因此坐牢。

00:53:09.480 --> 00:53:16.720
<v Newscaster>Video cameras, plants masquerading as drugs, and a message are what police found while serving a search warrant today.
<v Newscaster>摄像机、伪装成毒品的植物和一条留言，是警察今天在执行搜查证时发现的。

00:53:16.720 --> 00:53:37.700
<v Michael May>Barry had invited the local TV news to film the raid, and people got the point. Folks in west Texas didn't like the fact that cops busted in on a home on such flimsy evidence. And the police alienated even more residents when they threatened to subpoena the local paper, The Odessa American, to get the names of people posting anti-police comments on the newspaper's website.
<v Michael May>Barry 邀请了当地电视新闻来拍摄突袭，人们理解了重点。德克萨斯州西部的人们不喜欢警察在如此脆弱的证据下闯入民宅的事实。当警察威胁要传唤当地报纸《Odessa American》以获取在报纸网站上发布反警察评论的人的姓名时，警察甚至疏远了更多的居民。

00:53:37.700 --> 00:53:44.860
<v Newscaster>The reality show team out of Austin has been setting up the fake drug den for six months. But why all the trouble?
<v Newscaster>来自奥斯汀的现实秀团队已经设置这个假毒品窝点六个月了。但这所有的麻烦是为了什么？

00:53:44.860 --> 00:53:47.960
<v Barry Cooper>Get Yolanda Madden out of prison.
<v Barry Cooper>让 Yolanda Madden 出狱。

00:53:47.960 --> 00:54:22.850
<v Michael May>Raymond got the publicity he was after. A quarter million people watched the raid on YouTube. Newspapers started covering the Yolanda case. A year later, a judge released Yolanda from prison on the grounds that the prosecution had withheld documents. She's now awaiting retrial. For their part, the Odessa police department released a statement saying, the raid was a waste of law enforcement time and taxpayer money. The police threatened to charge Barry for staging the sting, but they never did.
<v Michael May>Raymond 得到了他想要的宣传。25 万人在 YouTube 上观看了这次突袭。报纸开始报道 Yolanda 案。一年后，一名法官以检方扣留文件为由将 Yolanda 从监狱释放。她现在正在等待重审。至于 Odessa 警察局，他们发表声明称，这次突袭浪费了执法部门的时间和纳税人的钱。警察威胁要以策划圈套为由指控 Barry，但他们从未这样做过。

00:54:22.850 --> 00:54:44.940
<v Michael May>After Odessa, Barry went looking for more dirty cops to bust. Without Raymond's money to spend, these were pretty low-budget affairs. Barry dressed up a duffel bag to look like something a drug dealer would tote around, including a crack pipe and $45 in cash, hoping cops would find it and take the money. He did the sting three times. Then the police struck back.
<v Michael May>Odessa 之后，Barry 去寻找更多脏警察来抓捕。由于没有 Raymond 的钱可以花，这些都是低成本的事务。Barry 把一个行李袋装饰得像毒贩随身携带的东西，包括一个快克烟斗和 45 美元现金，希望警察会发现它并拿走钱。他做了三次这个圈套。然后警察进行了反击。

00:54:44.940 --> 00:55:19.640
<v Michael May>Five days after Barry posted a video on YouTube of a cop in Williamson County finding the $45 in the bag and pocketing it, Williamson County police arrested Barry. And they did something police almost never do. They raided his home on a misdemeanor charge. The charge was false report to a peace officer for a phone call made to police about one of the duffel bags. Barry's wife, Candi, his 14-year-old daughter, and 8-year-old stepson were home during the raid. Barry was in handcuffs, but he still knew how police think. So he was sure he knew what this was all about.
<v Michael May>在 Barry 在 YouTube 上发布一段 Williamson 县警察发现包里 45 美元并私吞的视频五天后，Williamson 县警察逮捕了 Barry。而且他们做了一件警察几乎从不做的事。他们以轻罪指控突袭了他的家。指控是向和平官员做虚假报告，因为对有关其中一个行李袋的电话拨打了警察。Barry 的妻子 Candi、14 岁的女儿和 8 岁的继子在突袭时都在家。Barry 被戴上手铐，但他仍然知道警察是怎么想的。所以他确信他知道这是关于什么的。

00:55:19.640 --> 00:56:01.230
<v Barry Cooper>And I explained to them, after they had pointed those guns at my wife and then walked me into the house, I said, "Before any of you mother [BLEEP] are going to search any bit of my house, you're going to have to listen to me." And one of them tried to quiet me down. I said, "Mother [BLEEP], are you kidding me? This is a misdemeanor raid. I'm in handcuffs. I'm going to have my moment, or I'm not cooperating." So they stood down. And I went one by one, shaming every one of them, female officers and male officers, explaining to them that we were non-violent, that this was activism, that we know this is retaliation.
<v Barry Cooper>在他们把枪指着我妻子然后把我带进屋子后，我向他们解释说：“在你们这些混蛋去搜查我房子的任何部分之前，你们得听我说。”其中一个试图让我安静下来。我说：“混蛋，你在开玩笑吗？这是一次轻罪突袭。我戴着手铐。我要有我的时刻，否则我不合作。”所以他们撤退了。我一个接一个地羞辱了他们中的每一个，女警官和男警官，向他们解释说我们是非暴力的，这是一场激进主义活动，我们知道这是报复。

00:56:01.230 --> 00:56:24.160
<v Michael May>The police included narcotics officers who obviously hoped to find drugs in the house. They found a little pot, only enough for a misdemeanor possession charge. The police also referred Barry and Candi to Child Protective Services, charging that they were providing their teenage daughter with pot. After a visit, CPS dismissed the case. But the investigation took its toll.
<v Michael May>警察包括显然希望在屋里找到毒品的缉毒警察。他们找到了一点大麻，只够轻罪持有指控。警察还将 Barry 和 Candi 转介给儿童保护服务中心（CPS），指控他们正在给十几岁的女儿提供大麻。访问后，CPS 驳回了此案。但这起调查带来了损失。

00:56:24.160 --> 00:56:37.790
<v Michael May>Two weeks ago, Zach, Barry's 8-year-old stepson, visited his father for spring break and still hasn't been returned to Candi and Barry. Zach's father heard about the raid and filed for custody. Here's Barry.
<v Michael May>两周前，Barry 8 岁的继子 Zach 去看望他的父亲过春假，至今尚未归还给 Candi 和 Barry。Zach 的父亲听说了这次突袭并申请了监护权。这是 Barry。

00:56:37.790 --> 00:57:09.060
<v Barry Cooper>Our son being taken from us was so hard that, for the safety of my family, we decided that we're not going to do any more cop stings. You know, I was expecting-- I never would have done those bag drops, if I would have known it would have led to this.
<v Barry Cooper>我们的儿子被从我们身边带走太难了，以至于为了我家人的安全，我们决定不再做任何警察圈套了。你知道，如果我知道这会导致这种情况，我绝不会做那些放包的行为。

00:57:09.060 --> 00:57:30.740
<v Michael May>KopBusters was over. Barry had thought his first-hand knowledge of the system would keep him a step ahead. But the cops didn't need an elaborate ruse to sting Barry. They didn't need to plan for months and set a trap and get it all on video. They just needed a reason to come bursting in the front door.
<v Michael May>KopBusters 结束了。Barry 曾以为他对系统的第一手了解会让他领先一步。但警察不需要精心设计的诡计来惩罚 Barry。他们不需要策划几个月并设置陷阱并全部拍摄视频。他们只需要一个冲进前门的理由。

00:57:30.740 --> 00:57:38.800
<v Ira Glass>Michael May is the culture editor at The Texas Observer, whose website, texasobserver.org, is linking to all of the YouTube videos of Barry's police raids.
<v Ira Glass>Michael May 是《德克萨斯观察家》（The Texas Observer）的文化编辑，其网站 texasobserver.org 链接到了 Barry 警察突袭的所有 YouTube 视频。

